joining us once again on the programme  the minister responsible for the  vaccines rollout nadine zaha mr zawi  good to see you this morning um  and plenty of course to discuss about  the ongoing ongoing rollout but  let's not let the health secretary be  the only person that gets congratulated  on this program  we hit that 15 million target as the  minister minister responsible you've  clearly played a big part in that so  well done  thank you very much neil and it's an  incredible team  that has delivered that target not least  brigadier phil prosser and 101 logistics  brigade but of course the nhs the gps  the nurses the pharmacists  the volunteers the army of vaccinators  and of course the volunteers  marshaling people making sure those four  cohorts those most vulnerable people uh  uh were offered the vaccine  and uh were able to get through vaccine  sites in a smooth way as possible so  i stand on the shoulders of of heroes  and i feel in many ways  like a bit of an imposter but we we  focus on making sure now we deliver the  next target  which is we offer the vaccine to  all five to nine uh cohorts so that's um  in total we'll be at 32 million people  with the first dose and of course  the second dose this week is the first  sweet meal where  we've been able to with the supply  stabilizing  send out the schedules over the next  three weeks for people to begin  uh of course to deliver for cohort five  and six but also  to book in the second doses which will  begin in hospital hubs uh  uh in the final week of february and  then in  uh primary care and gps from the first  of march onwards  um can we then minister perhaps talk  just a little about a subject that i  know  is concerning you've you vaccine  hesitancy an interesting  piece of information that did not cross  my radar i have to admit  that some people within the the black  and minority ethnic asian minority  ethnic communities are saying that  they are concerned about taking the  vaccine because they're concerned  that it may affect fertility they're a  concern that it may affect them  if they are pregnant can you speak  directly to that and provide some some  reassurances  absolutely uh there is a tsunami of  disinformation misinformation which  we have a unit across government that is  dealing with the  uh uh technology platforms to take down  some of this fake news  uh in terms of effects on fertility it  does not  in terms of a pregnancy you can have  your vaccine  of course once you've had a discussion  with your clinician to make sure that  your own  um you know physiology allows you to do  that  it's one of the issues that simon  stevens the chief executive of the nhs  addressed last night which is  this sort of the pandemic itself and  then a pandemic of misinformation  or as i just referred to a tsunami of  misinformation  on social platforms and it's very  important that we continue  to deliver accurate information in  people's  own languages but of course in their  communities we're translating everything  into  20 languages from arabic to farsi to  hindu to  polish across the board we have to reach  those hard-to-reach groups because  although  and you've heard me say this before  vaccine positivity in the uk  so people adults saying they will take  the vaccine  is at 89 of the adult population  the 11 that are vaccine hesitant or  have questions uh skew heavily towards  uh some of the ethnic communities the  black and afro-caribbean communities the  indian and pakistani and bangladeshi  communities  and we are working with those community  leaders we've put 25 million pounds to  work  through local government and of course  most importantly i i  published the uptake strategy on  saturday  sharing data with local government the  nhs  and local government working hand in  hand to make sure we reach  those groups with the information and of  course  the access one of the things we found  neil is actually  a lot of people want easier access to be  able to go and get vaccinated  so we are assessing lots of pop-up sites  uh at least 200 new faith group  sites have come forward and we're  looking at those as well we're already  vaccinating in  the hickman center in batley that the pm  visited and of course  their bass mosque in birmingham and  cathedrals around the country  it's about access that i think we will  be our focus on that  and data sharing will hopefully get us  to the high levels of  acceptance from black and ethnic  minority communities  those those faith centers being used for  vaccination i'm sure  will advance the the campaign against  disinformation  but again a suggestion a very specific  suggestion coming from within the muslim  community  is that there are perhaps significant  numbers of people not taking  the first dose of the vaccine because  their  second dose would fall and it would fall  in ramadan now  i don't know enough about islam to say  whether or not this is something that  would be prescribed by islam but clearly  there are concerns again speak very  specifically to this community if you  can minister  yeah very much so uh neil so  of course we make um already  plans for ramadan when people are  fasting  uh and make sure that as we do uh  with your other communities uh look into  their festivals uh purim is almost upon  us for the  jewish community and of course ramadan  is something that we've got to make sure  that the system adjusts to be able to  deal with that  but faith leaders in the muslim  community have come out  very clearly whether it's the east  london mosque or the abbas  mosque in birmingham and said that  actually you have to come forward and  take your vaccine  it protects you we know that uh bamey  communities  are disproportionately impacted by this  virus so  come forward and take the first dose and  we'll make sure that  we will accommodate any issues around  ramadan or any other religious festival  to make sure that you can get your  second dose safely and in the correct  way  good to hear minister um i i wonder if  we can talk about some of the measures  that were  were introduced pretty much first in a  response to kovid 19 social distancing  and mask wearing a suggestion picked up  by our deputy political editor sam coats  you know that the scientists advising  the government are wanting to see those  measures kept in the for the  for the foreseeable future are you  minded  to see that happen i mean i suppose that  the point is that  you know people have been hearing  ministers talking about a return to  normality  for many people social distancing not  being able to hug your friends  wearing a face mask that doesn't fall  within  plenty of people's definition of of  normal  you you're absolutely right we are all  pro-social animals  um you know it's in our dna that we  want to socially interact we want to hug  our children our grandchildren  uh our our friends uh i would say this  we have to be data driven  rather than just date driven you know we  have to follow the  the evidence um from uh sage  from the scientists to make sure that  you know if  we are able to control this virus better  obviously our main  um you know our way out of this pandemic  is  our vaccination program which we are  we're making sure that we deliver  as quickly as possible protecting the  most vulnerable and then moving to the  whole of the adult population you know  we want to be able to  uh by september have offered the vaccine  to the whole of the adult population  but with that level of vaccination  which hopefully we'll see the data on uh  infection rates coming down  transmission there's some good data from  oxford but we're uh that has to be  peer-reviewed and we wait for our own  data from the  public health england uh surveys that  are being conducted  but with that surge testing  tracing and isolation and any other  measures whether it's mass squaring  or social distancing i think we have to  be driven by the data  rather than just dates uh that's that's  my message  to to uh your viewers is if we look at  the evidence  and share that evidence uh people are  are you know  able to make those decisions uh for  themselves and i think that's the right  way  to do that the prime minister will be  saying more on this on the 22nd of  february in terms of the roadmap back to  uh reopening schools hopefully on the  8th of march and then gradually  reopening our economy  but it has to be data driven evidence  driven rather than just  relying on on what we all want to see  because we're all anxious to want to get  our lives back and  our economy uh back on its feet as  quickly as possible  yeah and again this this kind of all  takes us back to to the prime minister  repeating yesterday something that he  said before the case numbers are going  to be incredibly  important in determining which measures  are relaxed and so on  i i suppose the question there is that  we are testing more than we ever have  done which is  undeniably going to register more cases  just because of that i mean if we look  back to when case numbers were low  last summer july the 4th when the pubs  reopened it was 624 cases  that we were registering with far fewer  tests  going on than there are now so i'm just  wondering what kind of level do we need  to bring it down to  before we start talking about what we're  going to be hearing you know  next monday well i think you're  you're right to look at whether it's  case numbers and  you're absolutely right to say that we  are now both in terms of capacity  at about 800 000 pcr capacity  tests a day uh and of course the level  of testing you know  we're we're almost touching that on  average a week about  six to seven hundred thousand tests uh a  day  uh per week as well as of course many  many  uh uh more uh lateral flow tests through  local government through  businesses uh uh as well as  uh people in the nhs and and in the  healthcare system having those  twice a week tests to make sure that we  remain on top of the virus but again the  prime minister's right to say  that where we are today uh in terms of  number of people in hospital  in terms of uh case numbers per day it's  still  far too high and we want to make sure we  bring that right down but i'm  you know i wouldn't want to speculate on  this  until we see more data and of course  ask those who are expert the the  scientists  the sage and of course the uh uh  chief medical officer to look at this  and to advise us accordingly  and be data driven we all want the same  thing that we want  to make sure we reopen the economy as  quickly as possible  but of course make sure that it is  sustainable that's the important thing  uh hence why we've got such a strong  vaccination  uh deployment campaign but also the  prime minister's already asked us  to think about not just this vaccination  deployment  but what will happen if we need a  booster in the autumn which we're  planning  for and of course then annual  vaccination program  uh for future years if we have to uh  deliver that if the virus be you know  has mutated to to a level where we need  vaccine variants and of course annual  uh vaccination programs all of that will  be  uh discussed in the mix and of course uh  the pm will set out  at the road map on the 22nd of february  based on the level just  just interrupt sorry to interrupt  minister but just one thing that the  prime minister did mention yes the  lateral flow  test to essentially enjoy a night out  now there are a couple of problems with  that obviously case numbers are far too  high  for mass use of lateral flow also are we  really going to be testing people  for access when there are so many false  positives  well so false negatives i should say yes  and  i got your uh point absolutely right so  i think  uh what the prime minister actually said  is it will be a combination a  combination  of the national vaccination program  becoming you know really successful  reaching that 89 of the adult population  where currently according to all the  surveys including the office for  national statistics survey  that uh you know 85 percent of the  adults say they will take the vaccine  the latest stats i saw i think from  ipsos mori said 89 said they will take  the vaccine  so what he was talking about is a world  where  we have you know the adult population  vaccinated  uh to those high levels and then rapid  testing technology  where you can get a quick test done and  then be able to use that as a way of  reopening  whether it be theaters or the rest of  the economy

